564 0 obj
<</ActualText(Multiplayer AI workspace where teams co-pilot autonomous workers and authorize by multisig wallet. )/K 1/P 578 0 R/Pg 592 0 R/S/Span>>
endobj
565 0 obj
<</ActualText(I. Executive Overview)/K 2/P 579 0 R/Pg 592 0 R/S/Span>>
endobj
566 0 obj
<</ActualText(The defining productivity platforms of the last two decades won by converting single-player tools into collaborative environments - Google Docs replaced Word, and Figma disrupted Photoshop by enabling real-time teamwork.\nToday, AI represents the most pow\
erful efficiency engine in modern enterprise, yet it remains completely trapped in an isolated, single-player paradigm. \nOnforce unlocks the multiplayer era for AI, transforming isolated agent space and prompts into a shared, governed execution canvas wh\
ere teams and autonomous agents co-pilot high-value business operations together in real time.)/K 3/P 580 0 R/Pg 592 0 R/S/Span>>
endobj
567 0 obj
<</ActualText(II. Problem Statement)/K 4/P 581 0 R/Pg 592 0 R/S/Span>>
endobj
568 0 obj
<</ActualText(SMB, and modern teams want AI agents to execute complete workflows, not just generate text. However, agentic deployment hits a structural barrier: The Autonomy vs. Control Paradox.)/K 5/P 582 0 R/Pg 592 0 R/S/Span>>
endobj
569 0 obj
<</ActualText(The Painful Workflow\nContext Fragmentation: Company knowledge is trapped in disconnected tools. Agents lack unified, real-time workspace memory.\nSingle-Player Isolation: Teammates prompt separate AI bots independently, producing duplicated work and unco\
ordinated outputs.\nThe Execution Wall: When an agent drafts a vendor payment or generates an enterprise contract, execution halts. Humans must manually review and copy data into third-party portals.)/K 6/P 583 0 R/Pg 592 0 R/S/Span>>
endobj
570 0 obj
<</ActualText(III. Target Customers \(Underserved SMEs and modern teams\))/K 7/P 584 0 R/Pg 592 0 R/S/Span>>
endobj
571 0 obj
<</ActualText(Small and Mid-sized Enterprises \(SMEs\) are adopting AI faster than large enterprises, but hit a severe operational ceiling due to resource constraints:\nHigh SME AI Adoption \(62%79% Adoption Rate\): Over 60% of SMEs actively experiment with or deploy \
AI tools to streamline operational workflows.\nThe Missing DevSecOps Infrastructure \(94% Lack Dedicated Security Teams\): 94% of SMEs lack in-house security engineering or DevSecOps teams to build custom access control frameworks, leaving them unable to \
grant AI agents direct execution credentials.\nThe 40% Project Failure & Governance Barrier: Gartner predicts that over 40% of agentic AI projects will be canceled or decommissioned by 2027 due to governance gaps, inadequate risk controls, and unmanaged a\
ccess boundaries identified after deployment.\nThe Manual Productivity Tax \(15+ Hours Lost Weekly\): Because SMEs cannot afford complex enterprise identity platforms \(IAM\), ops managers waste 15+ hours per week per operator acting as manual bridgesrev\
iewing agent outputs and re-entering data into external banking, crypto, or client portals.)/K 8/P 585 0 R/Pg 592 0 R/S/Span>>
endobj
572 0 obj
<</ActualText(IV. Market Size  )/K 9/P 586 0 R/Pg 592 0 R/S/Span>>
endobj
573 0 obj
<</ActualText(Total Addressable Market \(TAM\)  $182.97 Billion \(by 2033, 49.6% CAGR\)\nGlobal AI Agents Market: Valued at $7.63B in 2025 and $10.9B in 2026, projected to surge to $182.97B by 2033 at a 49.6% CAGR. Driven by enterprise adoption of autonomous decision-\
making agents, automated workflows, and task-oriented systems.\nServiceable Addressable Market \(SAM\)  $12.06 Billion \(2026\) / $24.7 Billion \(by 2027\)\nSME AI Workflow & Multi-Agent Collaboration: The addressable segment of multi-agent systems, task\
-automation orchestration platforms, and SME AI workflow automation. Expanding at over 42.9% CAGR as small and mid-sized businesses shift from basic prompt tools to collaborative multi-agent execution frameworks.\nServiceable Obtainable Market \(SOM\)  $\
850 Million \(Initial Target Horizon\)\nHigh-Velocity FinOps & Governed Execution: Initial target market focused on Web3-native DAOs, tech-forward FinOps SMEs, and digital agencies deploying autonomous multi-agent workflows that require multi-approver gov\
ernance over payouts, contracts, and core data operations.)/K 10/P 574 0 R/Pg 592 0 R/S/Span>>
endobj
